{
  "description": "issue #976, the OTHER half of `spec/interaction-events.md:119-122`. That paragraph blocks handlers only \"until the read finishes\": while `input` waits the implementation \"MUST NOT run new OpenLogo instructions or event handler blocks until the read finishes or the program is cancelled\" — and then they are permitted again. Its twin `input-does-not-deliver-handlers` proves the first half (a key pending at tick 0 delivers NOTHING across the read); this fixture is the identical program and the identical pending key with one `wait 1` appended, so the single variable between them is whether the read has finished. Here the key fires: the stream carries the handler REGISTRATION `primitive` (seq 1), the read (seq 3), the answer `print` (seq 5), then the handler FIRING `instruction` at seq 7 — discriminated by its `handler` payload (issue #954), which a registration never carries — and its `print \"handler ran\"`. The studio shipped the stricter reading at #952, refusing delivery for the rest of any chain that had asked a question; that is what #976 removes, and this fixture is what says the permissive behaviour is the conformant one rather than a studio preference.",
